## Proof

**Claim.** For a closed $n$-dimensional manifold $M$ and a point $x \in M$,
$$H_k(M, M - \{x\}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = n, \\ 0 & k \neq n. \end{cases}$$
In particular, $H_n(M, M-\{x\}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$.

---

### Step 1. Reduction by excision to a local model

Since $M$ is an $n$-manifold, $x$ has an open neighborhood $U \subset M$ together with a homeomorphism $\varphi: U \xrightarrow{\;\sim\;} \mathbb{R}^n$ sending $x$ to $0$. Set $V = M - \{x\}$, so the pair is $(M, V)$, and $U \subset M$ with $\overline{U} \subset \operatorname{int}(M - (V - U)) = \operatorname{int}(U)$. The hypothesis of **excision** is satisfied: the closure of $M - U$ lies in the interior of $V$. Excising $M - U$ gives
$$H_k(M, M-\{x\}) \cong H_k(U, U - \{x\}).$$

### Step 2. Identify the local pair with $(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\})$

Via $\varphi$, the pair $(U, U - \{x\})$ is homeomorphic to $(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\})$. Hence
$$H_k(M, M-\{x\}) \cong H_k(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}).$$

### Step 3. Use the long exact sequence of the pair

$\mathbb{R}^n$ is contractible, so $H_k(\mathbb{R}^n) = 0$ for $k \geq 1$ and $H_0(\mathbb{R}^n) \cong \mathbb{Z}$. The long exact sequence of the pair $(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\})$ gives, for $k \geq 2$,
$$0 = H_k(\mathbb{R}^n) \to H_k(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to H_{k-1}(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to H_{k-1}(\mathbb{R}^n) = 0,$$
so
$$H_k(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \cong H_{k-1}(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}), \qquad k \geq 2.$$

For $k = 1$, the relevant segment is
$$0 \to H_1(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to H_0(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to H_0(\mathbb{R}^n) \to H_0(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to 0.$$
Since $\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}$ is path-connected for $n \geq 2$, the map $H_0(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \to H_0(\mathbb{R}^n)$ is an isomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}$, giving $H_1(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) = 0$ and $H_0(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) = 0$ for $n \geq 2$. (For $n = 1$, $\mathbb{R} - \{0\}$ has two components, and one checks $H_1(\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{R}-\{0\}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$, consistent with the formula below.)

### Step 4. Compute $H_{k-1}(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\})$

$\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}$ deformation retracts onto $S^{n-1}$ via $v \mapsto v/\|v\|$. Therefore
$$H_{k-1}(\mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \cong H_{k-1}(S^{n-1}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k - 1 = 0 \text{ or } k - 1 = n - 1, \\ 0 & \text{otherwise}. \end{cases}$$

Combining with Step 3 (and noting the $k-1 = 0$ contribution is killed by the reduced-homology correction at the bottom of the exact sequence), we obtain
$$H_k(\mathbb{R}^n, \mathbb{R}^n - \{0\}) \cong \widetilde{H}_{k-1}(S^{n-1}) \cong \begin{cases} \mathbb{Z} & k = n, \\ 0 & k \neq n. \end{cases}$$

### Step 5. Conclusion

Combining the isomorphisms from Steps 1–4,
$$\boxed{H_n(M, M - \{x\}) \cong \mathbb{Z}}$$
and $H_k(M, M - \{x\}) = 0$ for $k \neq n$.

**Interpretation.** This group is the **local homology** of $M$ at $x$. It is independent of the choice of $x$ and of the global topology of $M$; it depends only on the dimension $n$. A generator of $H_n(M, M-\{x\}) \cong \mathbb{Z}$ is the **local orientation class** at $x$, and the data of compatible generators for all $x$ is equivalent to an orientation of $M$. The fact that this local group is $\mathbb{Z}$ in degree $n$ and zero otherwise is precisely the homological characterization of $M$ being an $n$-manifold.

